    ("jamb_eng_2013_69", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Tinu likes apples __ she does not like oranges.", "or", "for", "so", "but", 3, "Adversative conjunction 'but' coordinates contrasting clauses."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_70", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "The students had a __ on Independence Day.", "march past", "match pass", "march pass", "match past", 0, "A ceremonial parade of students or military units is a 'march past'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_71", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Do you mind __ another hour or two?", "to wait", "to have waited", "wait", "waiting", 3, "The polite phrase 'Do you mind...' is grammatically complemented by a gerund: 'waiting'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_72", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "The continuous rain has really __ the soil.", "melted up", "mopped up", "satiated", "saturated", 3, "Soil soaked with water to its maximum holding capacity is 'saturated'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_73", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "The police described the boy as being __ hand.", "on by", "up to", "over at", "out of", 3, "Idiom 'out of hand' means unruly, undisciplined, or beyond control."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_74", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "It was very easy for the two political parties to form a __ government.", "co-operative", "colonial", "collusion", "coalition", 3, "An alliance of distinct political parties forming a joint administration is a 'coalition government'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_75", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "All farmers were encouraged __ carry out fumigation on their farms.", "to", "from", "in", "with", 0, "Passive structure 'were encouraged' takes an infinitive: 'to carry out'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_76", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "There are lots of __ in the park.", "luxury buses moving fast", "luxury buses fast moving", "moving fast luxury buses", "fast-moving luxury buses", 3, "Compound participial adjectives precede the noun: 'fast-moving luxury buses'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_77", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Yours is to command, __ is to obey.", "their", "theirs", "theirs'", "their's", 1, "Possessive pronoun 'theirs' functions as a subject pronoun without an apostrophe."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_78", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Local governments are authorized to pass __.", "bye's-law", "bye-law", "bye-laws", "byes'-laws", 2, "Plural noun form for local municipal statutes is 'bye-laws'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_79", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Umar: I have never visited the dentist. Aliyu: __", "neither have I", "I also never", "neither myself", "I myself haven't", 0, "Negative agreement with a present perfect statement is formed with 'neither + auxiliary + subject': 'neither have I'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_80", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Usman would have won the race __.", "if he had run faster faster", "although he ran faster", "only if he could run fast", "if he had run faster", 3, "Third conditional structure: 'would have + past participle' requires 'if + past perfect': 'if he had run faster'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_81", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "My father told me to take the money from __ offers it.", "ever who", "whoever", "whomever", "whomsoever", 1, "Subject of the subordinate clause verb 'offers' requires the subjective pronoun 'whoever'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_82", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "Our teacher defined __ as the killing of one's mother.", "patriarch", "matricide", "matriarch", "patricide", 1, "'Matricide' is the murder of one's mother (patricide is murder of father)."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_83", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "If you are confused __ anything, phone my office.", "about", "for", "of", "with", 0, "The adjective 'confused' takes the preposition 'about'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_84", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "We have a family mutiny __ our hands.", "from", "of", "on", "for", 2, "The established prepositional idiom is 'on our hands'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_85", "English Language", "Grammar and Structure", "2013", "We should try to help __.", "the less fortunate", "this less fortunate", "the less fortunate person", "less fortunate.", 0, "Substantive collective adjective with definite article 'the less fortunate'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_86", "English Language", "Oral English: Vowels", "2013", "Choose the option with the same vowel sound as in 'glasier' (/eɪ/):", "gleam", "flat", "feign", "glass", 2, "'Glasier' / 'glazier' has the diphthong /eɪ/, identical to 'feign'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_87", "English Language", "Oral English: Vowels", "2013", "Choose the option with the same vowel sound as in 'laud' (/ɔː/):", "lavatory", "loud", "lathe", "core", 3, "'Laud' features the long open-mid back rounded vowel /ɔː/, which rhymes with 'core'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_88", "English Language", "Oral English: Vowels", "2013", "Choose the option with the same vowel sound as in 'coma' (/əʊ/):", "colonel", "cogent", "come", "comma", 1, "The initial syllable in 'coma' has the diphthong /əʊ/, exactly as in 'cogent'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_89", "English Language", "Oral English: Consonants", "2013", "Choose the option with the same consonant sound as in 'lose' (/z/):", "mouse", "nurse", "noise", "horse", 2, "The 's' in 'lose' is pronounced as voiced alveolar fricative /z/, matching 'noise'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_90", "English Language", "Oral English: Consonants", "2013", "Choose the option with the same consonant sound as in 'guitar' (/ɡ/):", "jam", "strange", "judge", "rogue", 3, "The 'g' in 'guitar' represents the voiced velar plosive /ɡ/, matching final 'gue' in 'rogue'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_91", "English Language", "Oral English: Consonants", "2013", "Choose the option with the same consonant sound as in 'loose' (/s/):", "sell", "fuse", "close", "rouse", 0, "'Loose' ends with the voiceless alveolar fricative /s/, as in 'sell'."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_92", "English Language", "Oral English: Rhymes", "2013", "Choose the option that rhymes with 'rite':", "list", "wit", "wright", "rim", 2, "'Rite' (/raɪt/) is a perfect homophone and rhyme with 'wright' (/raɪt/)."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_93", "English Language", "Oral English: Rhymes", "2013", "Choose the option that rhymes with 'Joys':", "elbow", "pots", "boys", "stays", 2, "'Joys' (/dʒɔɪz/) rhymes with 'boys' (/bɔɪz/)."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_94", "English Language", "Oral English: Rhymes", "2013", "Choose the option that rhymes with 'Call':", "wall", "quail", "dull", "slate", 0, "'Call' (/kɔːl/) rhymes directly with 'wall' (/wɔːl/)."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_95", "English Language", "Oral English: Stress", "2013", "Choose the appropriate stress pattern: dedication", "dedicaTION", "deDIcation", "dediCAtion", "DEDication", 2, "Penultimate stress before suffix '-tion': de-di-CA-tion."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_96", "English Language", "Oral English: Stress", "2013", "Choose the appropriate stress pattern: international", "interNAtional", "internaTIONal", "INternational", "inTERnational", 0, "Antepenultimate stress on third syllable: in-ter-NA-tion-al."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_97", "English Language", "Oral English: Stress", "2013", "Choose the appropriate stress pattern: information", "inforMAtion", "inFORmation", "INformation", "infor-ma-TION", 0, "Penultimate stress before suffix '-tion': in-for-MA-tion."),
    ("jamb_eng_2013_98", "English Language", "Oral English: Emphatic Stress", "2013", "Adamu is leaving a CAR behind.", "What is Adamu leaving behind?", "Is Adamu driving the car in front?", "Who is leaving a car behind?", "Where is Adamu leaving a car?", 0, "Emphatic stress on CAR prompts the question questioning the specific object being left behind."),
